<section xml:id="Section-1">

             <title>Finishing Bases</title>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-BaseSize">
        <statement>
          <p>Every basis of a finite dimensional vector space has the same size.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-BaseSize"/></title>
          <p>Let <m>B_{1}</m> and <m>B_{2}</m> be two bases, and suppose for the sake of contradiction that <m>|B_{1}| \lt |B_{2}|</m>. 
          Let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>B_{2} \setminus B_{1}</m> of maximal size such that there exists <m>T \subseteq B_{1} \setminus B_{2}</m> 
          such that <m>|S| = |T|</m> and <m>(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S</m> is a basis. Such a set exists, since one can take <m>S = \emptyset</m>. 
          Note that</p>
          <p><md>|(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S| = |B_{1}| \lt |B_{2}|.</md></p>
          <p>Thus, there exists <m>\vec{v} \in B_{2} \setminus ((B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S)</m>. Then by the Steinitz lemma, 
          there exists <m>\vec{u} \in ((B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S) \setminus B_{2}</m> such that 
          <m>(B_{1} \setminus (T \cup \vec{u})) \cup S \cup \{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis, a contradiction to the maximality of <m>S</m>.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      </section>